---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleSchema from "../../components/ArticleSchema.astro";
import FaqSection from "../../components/FaqSection.astro";
import { site, description } from "../../content";
const path = "/guides/flow-collision-prevention";
const title = "Make Shopify journeys share one dispatch decision";
const desc = "Prevent conflicting promotions while keeping service events under explicit ownership.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is a frequency cap enough?",
    "answer": "No. It can limit contact volume without resolving contradictory offers or stale intent."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should cart always outrank welcome?",
    "answer": "It can be a useful proposed policy, but preserve advertised signup promises and evaluate the actual customer context."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can a suppression tag expire?",
    "answer": "Operational locks need a defined lifecycle. Their expiry must not override withdrawal or other enduring ineligibility."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a shared prompt prove collision handling?",
    "answer": "No. Inspect generated conditions and demonstrate dispatch behavior, event replay and failure paths in the actual integration."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Make Shopify journeys share one dispatch decision</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Prevent conflicting promotions while keeping service events under explicit ownership.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Inventory every message owner</h2><p>Include the ESP, popup sender, SMS app, subscription app, review app and Shopify notices. For each message record trigger, purpose, channel, eligibility, stop events, offer and owner. Frequency throttling limits volume; collision logic resolves contradictory intent. Two messages can conflict even when sent days apart if both promises remain active. This guide proposes an architecture, not a claim that a named ESP implements it automatically.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Define the policy before the tags</h2><p>Treat permission, withdrawal and applicable service classification as eligibility decisions before promotional priority. Then choose among eligible journeys using current customer intent and your commercial promise. A cart may outrank browse; a recent purchase should end stale acquisition reminders. A promised signup benefit may need preservation even while discretionary welcome content waits. Order confirmations belong to a separate service owner rather than a universal promotional priority stack.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>A proposed collision matrix</h2><p>Cart intent: stop stale browse reminders for the same purchase context and defer optional welcome promotions. Completed order: exit acquisition reminders and consider relevant post-purchase messaging only under its own eligibility rules. Active subscription: review replenishment and winback eligibility against actual status rather than a missing one-time order. Sale campaign: reconcile discretionary lifecycle offers with the approved campaign terms. Support escalation: consider a temporary promotional hold without silently blocking necessary account notices. Adapt these choices to the store; they are not measured winning priorities.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Model state with ownership and expiry</h2><p>Store journey identifier, customer identity, relevant cart/order, state, version and expiry instead of an unexplained permanent in-cart tag. Define who sets and clears each state on purchase, cancellation, timeout and manual intervention. An expiry prevents stale locks but cannot resurrect an opted-out subscriber. Do not assume every tool has atomic state updates, synchronous segments or a shared customer ID; make those gaps explicit in the implementation design.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Recheck immediately before dispatch</h2><p>Enrollment-time filters become stale while messages wait. Recheck permission, current order state, higher-priority intent and active offer before dispatch where supported. Use an idempotency key for repeated event processing and keep a record of the decision. If two senders cannot coordinate, assign one owner or simplify the overlap rather than relying on a weekly export for time-sensitive arbitration. A prompt describing suppression is not proof that the generated workflow enforces it.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Proposed event-order tests</h2><p>With controlled identities, enqueue welcome then cart, browse then cart, cart then purchase, and purchase then a delayed cart event. Replay an event and confirm only the intended action remains eligible. Test an unsubscribe while a message waits, a failed state-clear operation, an expired lock, a guest identity merge and two channels qualifying together. Capture event time, arrival time, decision time and message ID. No tests are reported as run here.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Coordinate offers across channels</h2><p>Choose a common offer owner even if email and SMS remain separate senders. Record outstanding promises and customer preferences in the dispatch decision. Do not assume email permission authorizes SMS; complete the separate channel review before enabling it. At checkout, verify the actual discount settings as well: Shopify combination permission is separate from whether two messages should be sent. See the discount guide for economics and redemption cases.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Handle an incident without another automatic send</h2><p>Pause affected promotional branches, identify recipients and outstanding codes, and preserve event and decision logs. Check whether a clarification is necessary and appropriate before contacting anyone again; another broadcast may compound confusion. Give support the approved resolution, honor customer promises and investigate stale state, replay, identity or ownership failures. Keep genuine service paths under their own operational controls. Restore only after the specific regression cases pass.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Maintain a change record and fallback</h2><p>Attach matrix version, owner and rollback behavior to each new journey or campaign. Review overlaps when an integration, trigger, offer or identity rule changes. For a sale, explicitly schedule restoration and reconcile old valid codes. Ask Sequenzy or another candidate to demonstrate dispatch-time exclusions, replay handling and audit exports using these cases. If the stack cannot make a reliable shared decision, reduce simultaneous journeys before increasing complexity.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify"><h2>Primary references and related decisions</h2><p>Primary guidance checked 24 November 2026; scope is limited to the points attributed above. Store configuration and jurisdiction-specific review remain necessary.</p><ul><li><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/discounts/discount-combinations">Shopify discount combinations</a></li></ul><p><a class="link" href="/guides/consent-compliance">Consent compliance</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/deliverability">Deliverability</a> · <a class="link" href="/guides/discount-discipline">Discount discipline</a></p></div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
